Subject: api: the vxlan lock test's "disjoint" acquirer was not disjoint

0012 fixed the leak, the message and the timeout, and the test still failed
4 of 10 batches of 20 runs. The timeout was never the problem: the acquirer
is genuinely BLOCKED, not slow, so 3s -> 30s only made each failure cost ten
times as much.

The FIXTURE is the bug. `disjoint` and `sameTarget` both used VNI 9999, so
they shared the HOST lock key. Keys are acquired in sorted order and the host
key sorts before the target key, so sameTarget takes host(9999) FIRST and
then blocks on the held target slot -- holding host(9999) the whole time.
This "disjoint" acquirer needs host(9999) too, so the two RACE for it, and
whenever the disjoint one loses it cannot proceed until release(), which the
test only reaches after that select. It was disjoint from `held` and
contending with a sibling the test introduced itself.

That is also why the rate looked mysterious: it is a goroutine race, so it
tracks scheduling. Isolated, the disjoint acquirer never waits more than 4ms
over 400 iterations; the full three-contender shape reproduces the block on
the first iteration.

Giving the disjoint acquirer a VNI no other contender uses restores the
property the test claims to check.

MEASURED, same tree, same command, 200 runs each:

  before   4 of 10 batches FAILED
  after    0 of 10 batches failed

POSITIVE CONTROL, and it needed a second attempt worth recording. Collapsing
vxlanCreateLockKeys to ONE literal panics on keys[1] in the dedup, so the
test goes red without reaching the assertion -- a red for the wrong reason is
as useless as a green that asserted nothing. Setting BOTH keys to the same
constant is the real pre-0010 global lock, and under it the test fails on the
disjoint assertion at 30s, then passes again when restored.

The implementation is UNTOUCHED and was never at fault: shardLockTable
releases its map mutex before blocking, the keys are sorted so there is no
ordering cycle, and disjoint keys hash to different files.

